sentences.text:
The learned counsel for the petitioner would submit that admittedly, the second respondent/defacto complainant and the petitioner are closely related to each other; that the first accused and the second respondent/defacto complainant are jointly claiming rights over the disputed property; that the second respondent/defacto complainant and her husband had filed O.S.No.197 of 2007 before the Sub Court, Palani, for partition, and the said suit is still pending trial;
that while so, the FIR, which culminated in the final report, was registered giving a criminal colour to the civil dispute and is an abuse of the process of law; and that the prosecution is mala fide and liable to be quashed.
4. The learned counsel for the second respondent/defacto complainant would oppose the prayer for quashing the final report and would submit that the pendency of the civil suit between the parties would not be a bar to lodging a complaint if the petitioners have committed a cognizable offence;
that the second respondent/defacto complainant and other witnesses would be able to establish the offences committed by the petitioners; and that the points raised by the petitioners cannot be adjudicated in this quash petition.

